## Artefact 1: Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (2024) – Utilitarianism

### Summary  
This 2024 update of the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry on utilitarianism explains the ethical theory in detail, focusing on its core principle of maximising overall happiness or wellbeing. The article outlines classical utilitarian thinkers such as Bentham and Mill, and discusses modern applications in digital and technological contexts. It highlights strengths of the theory, including its clear decision‑making framework, while also addressing criticisms such as the potential neglect of minority rights. The updated 2024 version includes contemporary examples involving AI decision‑making, automated systems, and data‑driven technologies, showing how utilitarian reasoning is increasingly applied to modern ICT dilemmas.

### Reflection  
I chose this artefact because utilitarianism is one of the foundational ethical theories discussed in the unit, and understanding it helps me evaluate ICT decisions that affect society. The article strengthened my understanding of how utilitarian reasoning can guide choices in areas such as automation, privacy, and AI deployment. It also helped me recognise the limitations of focusing solely on outcomes, especially when minority groups may be negatively affected. This artefact is meaningful because it connects directly to workshop discussions about ethical frameworks and shows how classical theories remain relevant in modern digital environments. It helped me develop a clearer approach to analysing the societal consequences of ICT decisions.

